ng a fresh one like the ERC20 benchmark does.\n\nBut wait, since the timing only measures the blocks and not the pre-pop reset, the comparison should still be fair. Let me think through what's actually happening here... I'm realizing there are some fundamental differences in how these benchmarks are set up. The MPT benchmark uses an in-memory test provider factory, while the SALT benchmark is writing to actual RocksDB on disk. And in the SALT implementation, every state update is doing a synchronous WAL write, which is a completely different I/O pattern than what the MPT benchmark is doing.\n\nBeyond that, there's the per-iteration reset cost for SALT—it has to reprocess millions of state entries and write them back to RocksDB before each iteration starts, which means the block cache is cold every time. And then there's the double fsync issue: SALT is syncing both the state update and the trie update separately, whereas MPT just commits once.\n\nLooking deeper, the EphemeralSaltState design requires reading back existing values from RocksDB for every operation to track history, which means constant range scans. Meanwhile, SALT is doing expensive elliptic curve math with Pedersen commitments for every state change, while MPT just hashes with Keccak—something that's heavily optimized in hardware.",
